# Cross-repo siblings cloned by setup-dev-env at workspace-lock-pinned
# revisions (see codetracer-specs/Testing/Cross-Repo-CI-Integration.md).
#
# This list is the set of `../../<repo>` entries `tests/config.nims` actually
# resolves, plus the repos those pull in transitively. It has to be COMPLETE:
# ci.yml ran a bare `actions/checkout@v4` with no `setup-dev-env` at all, so
# none of them were on disk and 25 of the 29 `just test-c` entries died on
# `cannot open file`. Measured on run 33226741540, all three runners:
#
#   src/isonim/core/platform.nim(4, 22) Error: cannot open file: nim_everywhere/platform
#
# Unlike isonim-tui, isonim's recipes do not pipe into `tee`, so `just` aborted
# on the first failing line and the jobs went honestly RED rather than falsely
# green -- every ci.yml run since 2026-08-17 has failed. The diagnosis was still
# opaque: one line naming a Nim module, nothing naming this file.
#
# `just check-siblings` now guards both halves -- that every name here is on
# disk, and that every `../../` path in `tests/config.nims` is named here. The
# second is the drift this file actually suffered: 8 paths, 4 declarations.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY EVERY ENTRY CARRIES AN EXPLICIT SHA, AND WHAT REMOVES THEM AGAIN
#
# The header above is the intended end state: bare names, revisions resolved
# from the per-commit workspace lock. isonim cannot reach it yet, because there
# is no lock to resolve against. `metacraft-manifests` holds exactly one isonim
# lock -- `locks/dev/isonim/1f8087c82865057ec94d89f51a0988b33f2ee404.xml`, an
# old commit -- and none for `dev`'s tip. On a `pull_request` event
# `clone-siblings.sh` probes the lock for the PR BASE sha, not the head, so a
# bare list here fails before cloning anything:
#
#   resolve-sibling-rev: no workspace lock found for isonim
#
# That is what ci-reprobuild.yml (already on `setup-dev-env`) has been dying of.
# Locks are written by the reprobuild pre-push gate and re-anchored onto
# mainline by `publish-workspace-lock`; isonim runs neither, so no commit of it
# acquires one. A hook-coverage gap upstream of this repo.
#
# An explicit ref makes that gap non-fatal, and this is a documented property of
# the action, not a workaround of it. `clone-siblings.sh` sets `NEED_LOCK=1`
# only for an entry with an EMPTY ref; `lock_unavailable()` is fatal only when
# `NEED_LOCK=1`, and otherwise downgrades to a `::warning::` and clones. With
# all eight pinned, nothing needs a revision FROM the lock, so nothing depends
# on one existing. Every sibling is still cloned -- none is dropped, skipped or
# made optional -- and the lock is still consulted and still reported on.
#
# The SHAs are not "current tips". They are the exact revisions of the eight
# sibling checkouts in the workspace where `just test-c` and `just test-js` were
# measured green (748 assertions, 0 failures, 0 `cannot open file`). That is
# precisely the set the pre-push gate would have written into the lock had it
# been able to run, so this file is standing in for the missing lock rather than
# inventing a different pinning. Each SHA was verified present upstream with
# `gh api repos/metacraft-labs/<repo>/commits/<sha>`, and each is an ancestor of
# that repo's `dev`.
#
# TO REVERT: once any isonim commit on `dev` carries a published workspace lock,
# delete the `=<sha>` from the seven metacraft siblings below and they resolve
# from it again. `nim-stew`'s pin is independent and outlives this one -- see its
# own note at the bottom.
#
# THE COST, STATED PLAINLY: while these pins stand, isonim CI tests against
# frozen siblings, so a breaking change in one of them will not turn isonim red.
# That is strictly better than the current state (every run red, zero signal),
# and strictly worse than the lock. It should not be left here indefinitely.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Directly imported by src/ and tests/.
