# nim-stew is reached only through nim-faststreams: `faststreams/inputs.nim`
# imports `stew/ptrops`. It is easy to conclude it is unneeded, because on a
# developer machine a nimble-installed `stew` (`~/.nimble/pkgs2/stew-0.5.0`)
# satisfies it and removing this sibling changes nothing. It is NOT satisfied
# on a runner: the flake dev shell installs no nimble packages and the repo
# vendors no stew. Verified with `NIMBLE_DIR` pointed at an empty directory --
# without this entry, `nim check tests/test_http_types.nim` fails with
# `nim-faststreams/faststreams/inputs.nim(3, 7) Error: cannot open file:
# stew/ptrops`; with it, clean.
#
# Pinned to an explicit revision rather than resolved from the workspace lock,
# for the same reason isonim-tui pins the tree-sitter grammars: `nim-stew` is
# not a member of the `isonim` project manifest, so a bare entry resolves to a
# workspace-membership gap instead of a clone. `metacraft-manifests` already
# carries `repos/nim-stew.toml` and `projects/codetracer.toml` already includes
# it; the durable fix is one line adding that include to `projects/isonim.toml`,
# after which this pin should be dropped for a bare `nim-stew`. The SHA is
# `master` @ 83eb115, which is also `status-im/master` -- i.e. upstream tip, not
# a fork divergence.
#
# This pin is INDEPENDENT of the seven above and outlives them: those come off
# the moment a workspace lock exists, this one only when `nim-stew` joins the
# project manifest. Note also that `repos/nim-stew.toml` declares
# `branch = "codetracer"` (currently cc40540), so even after the membership fix
# a bare entry would resolve to the fork branch, not to 83eb115 -- worth
# checking that is what isonim wants before dropping the `=`.
nim-stew=83eb1157963b7f49351dbdd858355fa990bbe23c
